## 1.6 Security Headers and SSL/TLS Information

We can inspect publicly available HTTP security headers and SSL/TLS certificate information to understand how a website is configured.

### A. Security Headers

HTTP security headers help protect web applications against certain classes of attacks.

We can inspect response headers using `curl`:

```
curl -I https://example.com
```

Common security headers include:

|Header|Purpose|
|---|---|
|`Strict-Transport-Security`|Enforces HTTPS connections.|
|`Content-Security-Policy`|Restricts the sources from which content can be loaded.|
|`X-Frame-Options`|Controls whether a page can be embedded in frames.|
|`X-Content-Type-Options`|Helps prevent MIME-type sniffing.|
|`Referrer-Policy`|Controls how much referrer information is sent.|

### B. SSL/TLS Certificate Information

SSL/TLS certificates can provide information about a website's identity and certificate configuration.

We can inspect a certificate using OpenSSL:

```
openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com
```

Useful certificate information includes:

- Subject.
    
- Issuer.
    
- Validity period.
    
- Subject Alternative Names (SANs).
    
- Certificate chain.
    
- TLS protocol and cipher information negotiated during the connection.
    

**Note:** Unlike passive searches of existing public records, these commands initiate a connection to the target. Treat them as lightweight active information gathering.

